    "# Project Proposal: Fuel Prices and Railway Passenger Demand in Europe\n",
    "\n",
    "**TIL6022 TIL Python Programming**\n",
    "\n",
    "**Group 8**\n",
    "- S.J. (Sem) Boonstra\n",
    "- S. (Stefanos) Demenegas\n",
    "- S. (Seongje) Lee\n",
    "- M.T. (Mika) Schuback\n",
    "- T.M. (Teodros) Semma\n",
    "\n",
    "**Date: 02.10.2026**\n",
    "\n",
    "## Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "Recent events in the middle east have had a huge international impact. The blockage of the strait of Hormuz by Iran for example has caused fuel prices to reach peak levels across the globe. Oil prices are known to be very sensitive to geopolitical changes. It is however not the first time that oil prices spike like they do now. Historically there have been many events when oil prices have suddenly peaked. \n",
    "\n",
    "Rapid fuel price inflations can lead to a difference in travel behaviour. For example, a spike in electric cars, or people shifting towards other modes of transport. This project aims to analyse how spikes in fuel prices compare to travel behaviour pattern changes for the European Union. To narrow down the scope the research investigates this for number of passengers using railway transport. Railway transport has been chosen, because it is a widely used mode of transport in the European union and additionally, it is considered the main competitor for car transport.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Research Question\n",
    "\n",
    "**Main Question:** How has railway passenger demand moved alongside fuel price changes in European countries between 2006 and 2025 and how do these patterns differ between countries?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sub-questions:** \n",
    "\n",
    "1. How did rail passenger demand develop during the main fuel price episodes (2008, 2014-2016, and 2022)?\n",
    "2. How strongly are year-on-year changes (quarterly) in real fuel prices and rail passenger demand correlated in each country?\n",
    "3. Does rail demand respond to fuel price changes immediately or with a delay of one or more quarters?\n",
    "4. Which countries show the strongest association and what characteristics do these countries share?\n",
    "\n",
    "## Data \n",
    "\n",
    "**Dataset** \n",
    "\n",
    "All data is publicly available and standarized across EU countries. The analysis uses quarterly data from 2006 to 2025. Rail passenger demand is expressed per inhabitant to account for differences in country size and population changes. Since population is only available annually, the same value is used for all four quarters of a year. Fuel prices are converted to real prices by dividing by the general price index so that long-term inflation does not distort the comparison.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Variable | Measure | Source | Frequency |\n",
    "| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n",
    "| Rail passenger demand | 1000 passengers (table rail_pa_quartal) | [Eurostat](https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/databrowser/view/rail_pa_quartal__custom_22991950/default/table) | Quarterly |\n",
    "| Population | Population on 1 January (table demo_pjan) | [Eurostat](https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/databrowser/view/demo_pjan__custom_22992121/default/table) | Annual |
    "| Fuel prices | Petrol and diesel pump prices incl. taxes | [EU Weekly Oil Bulletin](https://energy.ec.europa.eu/data-and-analysis/weekly-oil-bulletin_en#price-developments) | Weekly (averaged to quarters) |\n",
    "| General inflation | HICP all items | [Eurostat](https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/databrowser/view/prc_hicp_minr__custom_22993037/default/table) | Monthly (averaged to quarters) | \n",
    "\n",
    "**Geographical and temporal scale**\n",
    "\n",
    "The geographical scope of the study will cover EU Member States for which comparable railway passenger data are available. Countries without railway passenger services or sufficient data for the selected dependent variable will be excluded from the analysis. Based on the currently available datasets, the study is expected to include 22 EU member states. \n",
    "\n",
    "The study will primarily focus on the period 2006–2025. This period includes several major geopolitical events influencing fuel prices such as the 2008 oil crisis, several war conflicts and COVID-19.\n",
    "\n",
    "##  Analysis Pipeline \n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Data preparation:** download the data, format them into one dataset, average weekly and monthly data to quarters, and compute real fuel prices.\n",
    "2. **Handling rail demand seasonality:** use year-over-year growth rates to compare each quarter with the same quarter one year earlier.\n",
    "3. **Handling COVID-19:** correlations are calculated with and without 2020 and 2021\n",
    "4. **Descriptive overview:** visualization of real fuel prices and rail passenger-km per country with a closer look at the three fuel price events.\n",
    "5. **Correlation analysis:** correlations between year-over-year changes in real fuel prices and rail demand per country, including lags of zero to four quarters.\n",
    "6. **Sensitivity per country:** a simple log-log regression per country.\n",
    "7. **Country comparison:** countries are compared and grouped by the strength of their association and the results are discussed in light of differences in rail networks and national policies.\n"
   ]
